<Base title="About" description="Rio Gonzales started in computer engineering, built a career in HR, and is becoming an AI/ML engineer focused on persistent memory for AI.">
  <section class="page-head wrap">
    <p class="label accent">About</p>
    <h1>I took the long way to engineering</h1>
  </section>

  <div class="prose wrap">
    <p>
      I’m Rio Gonzales. I started college studying computer engineering, with the goal of becoming a hardware engineer.
      Life had other plans. I switched to Business Administration, and an internship led me into human resources.
    </p>

    <h2>What HR taught me</h2>
    <p>
      I built a career there. Today I’m an HR Manager and a SHRM Senior Certified Professional. Across the
      organizations I’ve worked in, I kept running into the same problem. People want to understand their role and how to do their best
      work. Training and development gets left to managers and teams, who end up feeling lost, overloaded and
      unsupported. And when someone leaves, what they know leaves with them.
    </p>
    <p>
      I earned a master’s degree in Business Analytics to make better decisions with data. The data kept pointing to
      the same place. That problem is what I’m now <a href="/projects/">building software</a> to address.
    </p>

    <h2>Always technical</h2>
    <p>
      The interest in technology and complex systems was there long before the career. As a child I read books about
      military vehicles and aircraft. As a teenager I built desktop computers. In college I built cryptocurrency mining
      rigs. These days I work on computers, cars and software projects.
    </p>
    <p>
      I’ve come to accept that this is how I’m wired, and that I need to follow it. Technical work feeds my soul.
    </p>

    <h2>Why AI memory</h2>
    <p>
      Every problem I saw in HR came back to knowledge that lived in people’s heads. AI models are good with language
      but forget everything between sessions. Persistent memory, the layer that lets an AI remember, update and forget
      safely, is where those two meet. Security comes first in everything I build.
    </p>

    <h2>Where I’m headed</h2>
    <p>
      I’m teaching myself AI/ML engineering through a structured curriculum: software engineering and math first, then
      deep learning, data systems, agents and MLOps, and finally durable, long-running agents with persistent memory.
      Each course ends in a public project with tests and measured results. I’m doing this while working full-time.
      The <a href="/journey/">Journey</a> page tracks where I am.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
